# OpenSpec Configuration — {{PROJECT_NAME}}
# Copy to: openspec/config.yaml
# Fill in {{PLACEHOLDER}} values for your project.

schema: spec-driven

context: |
  This repository is instrumented with Harness Engineering. The change workspace
  Harness Engineering is the parent lifecycle and OpenSpec is its child
  specification capability. A change is live only after init_change.py writes a
  schema_version 3 change.json with the canonical parent-child contract.
  Change-scoped OpenSpec status/instructions/validation/show operations must be
  invoked through dispatch_openspec.py. Never use a standalone /opsx:* workflow:
  Harness alone owns creation, state, approval, implementation, Sync, and
  Archive. OpenSpec output is guidance, not execution authorization.

  Tech stack: {{TECH_STACK}}
  Build: {{BUILD_SYSTEM}}
  Database: {{DATABASE}}
  Module structure:
    {{MODULE_1}} — {{MODULE_1_DESCRIPTION}}
    {{MODULE_2}} — {{MODULE_2_DESCRIPTION}}
    {{MODULE_3}} — {{MODULE_3_DESCRIPTION}}
  Conventions:
    {{CONVENTION_1}}
    {{CONVENTION_2}}
    {{CONVENTION_3}}

rules:
  proposal:
    - Reference specific module paths when listing impact
    - Capability names use kebab-case
    - Always list affected modules in Impact section with directory paths
    - Check openspec/specs/ for existing capabilities before declaring Modified Capabilities
    - State that this change must be owned by the Engineering lifecycle (init_change.py writes change.json); never leave an unmanaged OpenSpec change directory
  design:
    - Every decision must specify which module owns the implementation
    - Use numbered decisions (D1, D2...) with choice, reason, and alternatives
    - New types must follow project conventions (specify module and package)
    - A design becomes executable only after external approval (approve_design.py) binds the contract; drafting is not authorization to apply
  specs:
    - Use SHALL/MUST for normative requirements (never should/may)
    - Every requirement must have at least one Scenario with WHEN/THEN format
    - Scenarios must use exactly 4 hashtags (####)
    - Reference specific module names and package paths in requirements
    - Specs must satisfy the Engineering SPEC/EXECUTE gates (#### Scenario with WHEN/THEN per requirement), not only OpenSpec validation
  tasks:
    - Group tasks by module dependency order
    - Every task must be a checkbox with a stable id: "- [ ] T<number> Task description"
    - Create task-plan.json with matching stable task ids, dependencies, explicit non-glob write scopes, contract refs, acceptance, focused verification, parallel eligibility, and coordinator merge order
    - Independent parallel-eligible tasks MUST have disjoint write scopes; overlapping scopes require a dependency
    - Verification tasks must include fitness gate invocation
    - task-plan.json is the approval-bound parent DAG; tasks.md is its child progress projection and is not an independent execution authority
    - After every successful task Harness MUST run record_task_completion.py complete with the task run evidence; that operation, never a manual edit, changes the matching checkbox to "- [x]"
    - Coordinator integration MUST run the Engineering fitness gate and record execution/review evidence; implementation is authorized only when change.json is APPROVED with approval.json bound
